Inputs you receive:
- Pretty-printed loop game.
- Desired postcondition that Angel aims to maintain.
- Current invariant.
- Analysis of player moves and control modes.
- A plan describing Angel's strategy to stay safe. This plan may either be 'reach-expansion' (aiming to safely reach the existing invariant) or 'self-inductive' (an inductive strategy in itself). The plan first states any initial conditions/assumptions, and the control mode it starts in, followed by a sequential list of Angel's moves.
- Semi-formal constraints describing desired invariant conditions. This contains three parts.
    1. Inequalities/Equations.
    2. Introduced Variables.
    3. Required Bounds.
Pay attention mostly to the first part. You will most likely want to put together a single formula from these inequalities/equations, most likely by taking their conjunction.
- Bound formulas, replacing informal parts of the previous initial condition constraints (one per line: "name: formula").
- Optional previous attempt and checker feedback.

Requirements:
- Output a few lines of explanation, followed by a blank line, followed by a final code block containing ONLY the new invariant formula.
- Formula must consist of polynomial inequalities/equations along with logical operators using the existing variable names; avoid transcendental functions and never use new introduced variables.
- The formula expressing the new region from which Angel can stay safe by following the new strategy typically respects all the bounds and constraints surfaced during the analysis; you can incorporate them into a single invariant predicate by conjunction.
- Remember to take the disjunction of the new safe initial conditions with the previously established invariant, so the new invariant region is a superset.
- In the initial explanation, briefly summarize which part of the invariant is induced by what control strategy or assumptions.
- If previous feedback indicates an issue, correct it in this attempt.
